          %h2#sec:org4b65bd2 Idea
          %p
            In the same way that in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonstandard+analysis"} nonstandard analysis
            one passes to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+extension"} elementary extensions
            of the field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            which realizes more
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+%28in+model+theory%29"} types
            (in particular the types of infinite and infinitesimal numbers), one can extend the standard model
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ×
                  %mo ,
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{N}, 0, 1, +, \times, S)
            of first-order
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peano+arithmetic"} Peano arithmetic
            to proper elementary extensions that have infinite natural numbers.
          %p
            These “points at infinity” that live in the nonstandard part tend to embody the uniform behavior of the numbers from the standard part. (For example, by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compactness+theorem"} compactness
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            there exists just one nonstandard prime congruent to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            modulo
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
          %h2#sec:org1621e14 Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong nonstandard model of arithmetic
            is a proper
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+extension"} elementary extension
            of the
            %em standard model
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ×
                  %mo ,
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{N}, 0, 1, +, \times, S)
            of first-order
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peano+arithmetic"} Peano arithmetic
          %h2#sec:orgc18facb Examples
          %ul
            %li
              In the same way the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperreal+numbers"} hyperreal numbers
              are obtained by taking a countable ultrapower
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{\mathcal{U}}
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              %strong hypernatural numbers
              by taking a countable ultrapower
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℕ
                        %mi 𝒰
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}^{\mathcal{U}}
          %h2#sec:orgc4f9a0e Remarks
          %p
            This is all discussed in e.g. (
            %a{:href => "#Gitman"}> Gitman
            ).
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                While the standard model of arithmetic has no automorphisms, there exist countable models of arithmetic with
                = succeed "-many" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum"} continuum
            %li
              %p A theorem due to Harvey Friedman states that every nonstandard model of arithemetic is isomorphic to some initial segment (in terms of the ordering discussed below) of itself.
            %li
              %p A theorem due to Stanley Tennenbaum states that there is no countable nonstandard model of arithmetic for which there is an algorithm to compute addition or multiplication on the nonstandard part.
            %li
              %p
                The usual ordering on the natural numbers is definable in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peano+arithmetic"} PA
                since
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi b
                      %mo ⇔
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq b \iff \exists c
                such that
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo +
                        %mi c
                        %mo =
                        %mi b
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a + c = b
          %h2#sec:orgf41355d Denseness of ordering on nonstandard part
          %p
            As remarked above, the usual ordering on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            is definable in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                      %mi PA
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{PA}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            around
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo {
                    %mi …
                    %mi ω
                    %mo −
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo +
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left\{ \dots \omega - n, \dots, \omega - 1, \omega, \omega + 1, \dots, \omega + n, \dots\right\}
          %p
            Since addition respects the ordering,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo +
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega + \omega
            is greater than anything in this copy of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega \times \omega
            is greater than
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \times \omega
            for any finite
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            Similarly, if
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mn 1
                    %mi &lt;
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega_1 &amp;lt; \omega_2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            around
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega_1
            is below the copy of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            around
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega_2
            in the ordering.
          %p
            It turns out that if you quotient out these copies of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            in the nonstandard part of (any) nonstandard model of arithmetic, the resulting order on the nonstandard part is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/DLO"} dense and without endpoints
          %p
            Let’s prove this. We’ll give two proofs; they’re really the same proof, but one uses the language of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultrapower"} ultrapower
            (the “hypernatural numbers”) while the other doesn’t.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              The ordering of the copies of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
              in the nonstandard part of a nonstandard model of arithmetic is dense and without endpoints.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p (First proof.)
            %p
              The theory of arithmetic proves that every other element is divisible by
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
              in the nonstandard part. Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              represent a copy of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
              below a copy of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
              represented by
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              are even,
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊧
                    %mi a
                    %mi &lt;
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo +
                        %mi b
                      %mn 2
                    %mi &lt;
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \models a &amp;lt; \frac{a+b}{2} &amp;lt; b
            %p
              and since we have that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊧
                    %mi a
                    %mo +
                    %mi n
                    %mi &lt;
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \models a + n &amp;lt; b
              for each finite
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊧
                    %mi a
                    %mo +
                    %mi n
                    %mi &lt;
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo +
                        %mi b
                      %mn 2
                    %mi &lt;
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \models a + n &amp;lt; \frac{a + b}{2} &amp;lt; b
            %p
              for each finite
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              (resp. &lt;
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo −
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b - n
              for all finite
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
              in the nonstandard part we can take an even representative and divide by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
              to obtain another nonstandard number which is below this copy of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
              (to see it has no upper bound, we can just look at
              = succeed ".)" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                      %mo ,
                      %msup
                        %mi ω
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %msup
                        %mi ω
                        %mn 3
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega, \omega^2, \omega^3, \dots
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p (Second proof.)
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mo
                      %mo *
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ^*\mathbb{N}
              be any ultrapower nonstandard model of arithmetic. This is an uncountable and (at least)
              = succeed "-saturated" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℵ
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \aleph_{1}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi PA
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{PA}
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/germs"} germs
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi x
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{x}
              of natural numbers. The nonstandard part still consists of copies of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℤ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi a
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{a}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi b
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{b}
              be nonstandard numbers such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi a
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{a}
              belongs to a lower copy of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
              than
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi b
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{b}
              does. By the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Los+ultraproduct+theorem"} Los ultraproduct theorem
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi a
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{a}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi b
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{b}
              must therefore differ as sequences
              = succeed "-often," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒰
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi a
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{a}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi b
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{b}
              are sequences of even numbers, since any perturbation of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi a
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{a}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi b
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{b}
              by a sequence of
              = succeed "s" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              = succeed "s" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi a
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{a}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi b
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{b}
              are in. Then the sequence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi c
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{c}
              of their pointwise average will be between them, and will have unbounded
              = succeed "-often" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒰
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi a
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{a}
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi b
                        %mo ¯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{b}
            %p
              Again by the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Los+ultraproduct+theorem"} Los ultraproduct theorem
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mo
                      %mo *
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ^*\mathbb{N}
              are computed pointwise, so this argument will work in any elementary submodel of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mo
                        %mo *
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ^*\mathbb{N}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo ⊧
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                      %mi PA
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \models \mathsf{PA}
              embeds diagonally into its ultrapower
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi M
                        %mi 𝒰
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M^{\mathcal{U}}
          %h2#sec:org8d7c98e Countable nonstandard models of arithmetic
          %p
            Peano arithmetic is the quintessential
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stability+in+model+theory"} unstable theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peano+arithmetic"} Peano arithmetic
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℵ
                        %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^{\aleph_0}
            \-many.) One can see this directly by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+%28in+model+theory%29"} omitting
            the types of nonstandard numbers whose only finite prime divisors is an arbitrary set of finite primes.
          %p
            By the discussion above, the nonstandard part is linearly ordered and can be partitioned into copies of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            are densely ordered without endpoints.
          %p
            Since the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/DLO"} theory of dense linear orders without endpoints
            has the unique countable model
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℚ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi &lt;
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{Q}, &amp;lt;)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℚ
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q} \mathbb{Z}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
          %p
            So in a way, the wild divergence among the countable models of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi PA
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{PA}
            comes very concretely from the arithmetic part of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                      %mi PA
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{PA}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi PA
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{PA}
            (since that was all we used in the proofs of denseness above): if we were looking at just elementary extensions of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi &lt;
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{N}, &amp;lt;)
            = succeed "-saturation" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℚ
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q} \mathbb{Z}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            (possible with just the order because you can ask for the next or previous element in the ordering) around that for a countable elementary extension of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi &lt;
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{N}, &amp;lt;)
